macOS: %gs:0x20 keeps what PE code last wrote, instead of Wine's Tib.FiberData, across the crossings.

Patch 0009 gives the pthread QoS slot (%gs:0x20) to PE code while PE code runs and to the host while unix
code runs. On every return to PE code it wrote teb->Tib.FiberData into the slot, which is right for
kernelbase's fibers (0008 keeps that field and the slot in step at every switch) and wrong for a program
that runs its own fiber system and keeps its current fiber in %gs:0x20 itself, Boost.Context style: the
first syscall after a switch handed it Wine's value (NULL or a stale kernelbase fiber) and Microsoft
Flight Simulator 2024 died in its own "FiberError" assertion at BOOT_INIT after 40 frames (M4, macOS 27.0,
engine r18, 2026-10-08, gin/private/notes/msfs-2024-2026-10-08.md).

Now the value PE code last left in the slot is saved into the thread data (pe_fiber, 0x350) at every entry
to unix code (syscall dispatcher, unix-call dispatcher, user_mode_callback_return) and put back at every
return (both dispatchers' return paths, call_user_mode_callback). It starts as Tib.FiberData at thread
start. kernelbase's fibers are unchanged: they write both the field and the slot, and the slot is what
survives. The host QoS value is handled as before.

Why not the upstream gs-base swap (3a16aabbf5): tried on this branch first, it works for console and
GUI programs on both bitnesses but D3DMetal's PE DLLs call the host framework directly from PE code
(GPTK's unix_call_enter/exit pattern), which needs the host block in %gs during PE code, so Steam died in
pthread_setname_np with a NULL thread block the moment D3DMetal came in. Record in the note above.

Trial for the MSFS 2024 investigation, branch msfs-gsbase. Unverified when written.
